WEBVTT

00:00:01.740 --> 00:00:05.740
You will recall from our early lessons on tissues that you’re kept alive and moving

00:00:05.740 --> 00:00:10.040
by three types of muscle tissue: smooth, cardiac, and skeletal.

00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:14.160
Your smooth muscle tissue is found in the walls of all your hollow visceral organs,

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:19.380
like your stomach, and airways, and blood vessels, where it involuntarily and very usefully

00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:23.680
pushes fluid and other material around by contracting and relaxing, over and over.

00:00:23.860 --> 00:00:27.960
Your heart is so important that it gets its very own muscle tissue type -- cardiac muscle,

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:32.660
which looks striped, or striated, and also functions involuntarily to keep your blood

00:00:32.660 --> 00:00:34.320
pumping without you having to think about it.

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:38.080
But when you hear the word “muscle,” you probably think of the kind you see on Chris

00:00:38.080 --> 00:00:40.760
Evans when he first walks out of that machine in Captain America.

00:00:40.940 --> 00:00:46.060
And those types -- the ones you can see and feel and flex -- are your 640 skeletal muscles.

00:00:46.360 --> 00:00:50.200
They’re striated like cardiac muscle tissue, but they’re also mostly voluntary, meaning

00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:54.460
you have to think about using them and activate them with your somatic nervous system. Most of

00:00:54.460 --> 00:00:59.020
them attach to your skeleton, and create movement by pulling bones in different directions as they contract.

00:00:59.420 --> 00:01:03.620
Each one of your different skeletal muscles -- like your biceps brachii, or vastus lateralis

00:01:03.620 --> 00:01:08.560
or gluteus maximus -- is technically its own organ, made up mostly of muscle tissue, but

00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:11.280
also of connective tissue, blood vessels, and nerve fibers.
